            <ArticleTitle>Coronary steal with unstable angina secondary to a coronary artery fistula.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>542-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 62-year old man with progressive deterioration of stable angina presented with an unstable episode and was referred for diagnostic coronary angiography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Physical examination, electrocardiography, myocardial perfusion scan, coronary angiography, cardiac CT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Left coronary main stem to pulmonary artery fistula.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Coronary angiography, percutaneous coronary intervention, coil embolisation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
